CONTEXT
My site's visual signature is the iridescent ring nav button already built in the codebase (the prismatic conic-gradient ring component). Its look — spectral colors glowing like light refracted through a prism, with soft photographic bloom — is the color reference for the ENTIRE site. Right now the rest of the page uses color that clashes with it: the hero name gradient, the colored background streak lines, and the colored stat numbers all look like they came from different palettes. Your job: unify every colored element on the site into ONE professional prismatic palette so the whole page reads as a single high-quality piece, not decorated parts.

THE PALETTE — ONE DEFINITION, USED EVERYWHERE
1. Define a single spectral palette in :root as an ordered set of stops, in TRUE SPECTRAL ORDER (this is what makes prismatic color look professional instead of ugly):
   --spec-1: warm amber → --spec-2: orange-red → --spec-3: magenta → --spec-4: violet → --spec-5: blue → --spec-6: cyan.
   Tuning: high luminosity, medium-high saturation, all stops equal perceived brightness (verify in OKLCH — no stop may look darker or dirtier than its neighbors). Target the look of white light split by a prism: clean, luminous, expensive.
2. HARD RULE: no color anywhere on the site may exist outside this palette. Every gradient, glow, streak, underline, ring, and tinted number must sample these stops IN ORDER — never shuffled. Shuffled spectral stops are exactly what makes rainbow gradients look cheap.
3. Color is always applied AS LIGHT, never as paint: every colored element gets a subtle bloom (a blurred duplicate underneath at low opacity). Flat colored fills are forbidden.
4. Rebuild the existing ring button's gradient from these same variables so it and the site share one source of truth.

